  "query_preview": "..Near zonal flow currently in place across Arizona, \nwith shortwave ridging just upstream. PW values were in the 0.25-\n0.40 inch range across the forecast area, with dewpoints in the \nupper 30s to lower 40s. High cirrus level clouds were noted across \nmuch of southeast Arizona, but will shift northeast of the area by \nthe late morning hours. Otherwise, high pressure aloft will build in \nacross the desert southwest through Wednesday, with the upper flow \npattern turning increasingly southwest toward midweek. This will \nresult in dry conditions and warmer temperatures across southeast \nArizona into Thursday.  \n\nLate this week into the upcoming weekend the models show an active \npattern, with west coast troughing and southern stream moisture \nmoving into the southern half of the west coast and across the \nIntermountain West. There continues to be timing/track differences, \nwith the ECMWF now focusing more on the northern half of Arizona \ninto the Great Basin, while the GFS is focused further south across \nthe desert southwest. The GFS/ECMWF solutions have flipped over the \nlast 24 hours. Cluster Analysis is in good agreement with the west \ncoast troughing late this week/weekend, although most solutions keep \nthe largest height anomalies to our north/northwest. The EMC GEFS \nplumes across southeast Arizona have mean storm rainfall totals of \n0.45-0.65 inches, with a couple of solutions indicating 1.0+ inches. \nThe 24-hour Tucson rainfall NBM probabilities currently have a 38% \nprobability for 1+ inch and a 10% probability for 2+ inches. The \ncurrent NWS forecast for Tucson has 0.18 inches spanning Friday and \nSaturday, with 0.82 inches during the Sunday/Monday time frame and \nhigher totals in the mountains. This seems reasonable given the \nmodel differences at this time. Overall, the weather turns unsettled \nlate in the day Thursday, with a chance of showers and thunderstorms \nFriday and Saturday across southeast Arizona and then the potential \nfor subtropical moisture plume influence late Saturday into Monday.  \n\nHigh temperatures will be near normal today, warming to 2-4 degrees \nabove normal Tuesday through Thursday, then lowering to 4-6 degrees \nbelow normal Friday and Saturday.",
